  <id>ee1b6efd-1cc6-441c-a50e-edc50f5ac6a3</id>
  <name>RDBMS Key Automation Extensions</name>
  <designerReferences />
  <packageSettings />
  <packageExtensions>
    <packageExtension type="Domain Package" typeId="1a824508-4623-45d9-accc-f572091ade5a">
      <requiredPackages />
      <contextMenuOptions />
      <creationOptions />
      <scriptOptions />
      <typeOrder />
      <macros>
        <macro trigger="on-loaded">
          <dependencies>
            <dependency name="Key Automation Extensions Script" id="63db56c6-5e20-4a9b-974e-d305e4919901" />
          </dependencies>
          <script>executePackageOnLoaded(element);</script>
        </macro>
      </macros>
    </packageExtension>
  </packageExtensions>
  <elementSettings />
  <elementExtensions>
    <elementExtension type="Attribute" typeId="0090fb93-483e-41af-a11d-5ad2dc796adf">
      <mappingSettings />
      <macros>
        <macro trigger="on-changed">
          <dependencies>
            <dependency name="Key Automation Extensions Script" id="63db56c6-5e20-4a9b-974e-d305e4919901" />
          </dependencies>
          <script>executeAttributeOnChanged(element);</script>
        </macro>
        <macro trigger="on-deleted">
          <dependencies>
            <dependency name="Key Automation Extensions Script" id="63db56c6-5e20-4a9b-974e-d305e4919901" />
          </dependencies>
          <script>executeAttributeOnDeleted(element);</script>
        </macro>
      </macros>
      <rules>## Foreign Key attributes are auto-managed
When `Key Creation Mode` is Explicit, Foreign Key attributes for this Class are created/removed automatically based on its associations. Do not manually add or delete a Foreign Key attribute unless `Key Creation Mode` is set to "Manually add PKs and FKs" — otherwise it will be silently reverted. See the AI guidance on the `Association` type for when automatic FK generation does and doesn't trigger for 1-to-1 style associations.</rules>
    </elementExtension>
    <elementExtension type="Class" typeId="04e12b51-ed12-42a3-9667-a6aa81bb6d10">
      <contextMenuOptions>
        <runScript type="run-script">
          <text>Auto Manage Keys</text>
          <icon type="UrlImagePath" source="data:image/png;base64," />
          <dependencies>
            <dependency name="Key Automation Extensions Script" id="63db56c6-5e20-4a9b-974e-d305e4919901" />
          </dependencies>
          <script>executeClassAutoManageKeys(element);</script>
        </runScript>
      </contextMenuOptions>
      <typeOrder />
      <mappingSettings />
      <macros>
        <macro trigger="on-changed">
          <dependencies>
            <dependency name="Key Automation Extensions Script" id="63db56c6-5e20-4a9b-974e-d305e4919901" />
          </dependencies>
          <script>executeClassOnChanged(element);</script>
        </macro>
        <macro trigger="on-created">
          <dependencies>
            <dependency name="Key Automation Extensions Script" id="63db56c6-5e20-4a9b-974e-d305e4919901" />
          </dependencies>
          <script>executeClassOnCreated(element);</script>
        </macro>
      </macros>
      <rules>## Missing expected Foreign Key on this Class
Primary/Foreign Keys on this Class are auto-managed based on its associations (when `Key Creation Mode` is Explicit). If an expected FK attribute is missing for a 1-to-1 style association, it is very likely because the association was authored in the direction that doesn't qualify for automatic FK generation — see the AI guidance on the `Association` type for the exact rule — rather than a designer defect.</rules>
    </elementExtension>
  </elementExtensions>
  <associationSettings />
  <associationExtensions>
    <associationExtension type="Association" typeId="eaf9ed4e-0b61-4ac1-ba88-09f912c12087">
      <sourceEndExtension>
        <typeOrder />
        <typeReferenceExtension>
          <isRequired>true</isRequired>
          <targetTypes />
          <allowIsNavigable>inherit</allowIsNavigable>
          <allowIsNullable>inherit</allowIsNullable>
          <allowIsCollection>inherit</allowIsCollection>
        </typeReferenceExtension>
      </sourceEndExtension>
      <targetEndExtension>
        <typeReferenceExtension>
          <isRequired>true</isRequired>
          <targetTypes />
          <allowIsNavigable>inherit</allowIsNavigable>
          <allowIsNullable>inherit</allowIsNullable>
          <allowIsCollection>inherit</allowIsCollection>
        </typeReferenceExtension>
      </targetEndExtension>
      <macros />
      <rules>## Foreign Key Generation for 1-1 (non-collection) Associations

When `Key Creation Mode` is `Explicit` (the default), a Foreign Key attribute is only generated automatically for a non-collection (1-to-1 style) association when the association's **source** end multiplicity is `0..1`. The target end's multiplicity only controls whether the generated FK is nullable — it does NOT determine whether an FK gets created at all.

This means direction matters: whether an FK attribute appears is decided by which class you modeled as the association's *source*, not just by which end looks "optional". To get an FK on a specific class, create the association FROM that class TO the class it references, with that class's own (source) end set to `0..1`.

If you author the association the other way around (source end `1`, target end `0..1` or `1`), NO Foreign Key attribute will be created on either class — and no validation error or warning will indicate this. This is easy to fall into: e.g. modeling "Customer has zero-or-one Passport; Passport requires exactly one Customer" the natural way (Customer as source/`1`, Passport as target/`0..1`) produces no FK anywhere.

If you need the FK on the "wrong" side per this rule, or both ends must be mandatory (`1` -&gt; `1`), the automatic rule cannot produce it. In that case, switch the `Key Creation Mode` application setting to "Manually add PKs and FKs", then add the Attribute yourself and apply the `Foreign Key` stereotype (setting its `Association` property to the correct association end). Note: while `Key Creation Mode` remains `Explicit`, a manually-added attribute for a non-qualifying association will be silently deleted the next time the class is modified, because the automatic rule still does not recognize it as required.</rules>
    </associationExtension>
  </associationExtensions>
